  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Myths · MYTH·21</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Only Eat Carrion? The Omnivore Myth Examined</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <div class={`verdict verdict-${verdict}`}>
      <span class="verdict-label">Verdict: False</span>
      <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are generalist omnivores, not carrion specialists. Their diet spans invertebrates, small vertebrates, eggs, berries, grain, and human food waste — alongside carrion. Carrion is an important food source, particularly in winter and in open/arctic habitats, but diet studies consistently show it represents a fraction of total intake, not the whole. The myth of the carrion-only raven reflects their cultural association with death rather than their actual feeding ecology.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Ravens Actually Eat</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Diet studies from raven populations across the Holarctic document broad omnivory:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles, grasshoppers, caterpillars, earthworms, and other invertebrates are taken opportunistically year-round. In summer, invertebrates may dominate diet in some populations — ravens foraging over meadows and open ground actively search for invertebrate prey in a manner similar to rooks or magpies.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> voles, mice, shrews, lizards, frogs, and small birds are captured and consumed. Ravens are capable hunters of small mammals — they are documented catching voles in direct pursuit and striking birds flushed from vegetation.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs and nestlings:</strong> raven predation on seabird colonies (guillemot and puffin eggs), ground-nesting birds (grouse, lapwing), and other species' nests is well documented. Egg predation is a significant seasonal food source in areas with accessible nesting colonies.</li>
      <li><strong>Plant material:</strong> berries (rowan/Sorbus, blueberry/Vaccinium, crowberry/Empetrum) are consumed heavily in late summer. Grain from agricultural fields contributes to raven diet in lowland farming areas.</li>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> ungulate, livestock, and road-kill carrion is taken opportunistically and may dominate diet in winter when other prey is scarce. Ravens are adept at locating carcasses and use social information (following other ravens) to find food.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> refuse, food scraps, and cached human food represent a significant modern food source in areas with human habitation.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why the Myth Persists</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Several factors sustain the carrion-only perception:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cultural visibility:</strong> raven appearances at medieval gallows, battlefields, and slaughterhouses were conspicuous and memorable events. These high-profile encounters with the death-carrion context became emblematic even though they represented a narrow slice of actual raven feeding behaviour.</li>
      <li><strong>Literary reinforcement:</strong> from the Norse battlefield-raven to Poe's "Nevermore", literary representations of ravens focus almost exclusively on the death and carrion context. This creates a feedback loop — the cultural image selects for the death association and omits the invertebrate-foraging, berry-eating raven entirely.</li>
      <li><strong>Confusion with vultures:</strong> the raven's association with death leads to confusion with vultures, which are obligate carrion specialists. Ravens share the feeding site with vultures at large carcasses but are taxonomically, ecologically, and behaviourally very different.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/questions/what-do-ravens-eat/">What do ravens eat?</a></li>
